  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nA longwave trough is positioned over the eastern CONUS, with \nridging present over the western CONUS. The local area is \nsandwiched between these two features with northerly winds and \nCAA bringing cooler temperatures to the forecast. High \ntemperatures this afternoon will struggle to reach 50F across \nnorthern MO as a few low-to-mid level clouds float across the \narea today. Surface high pressure builds into the Northern and \nCentral Plains this afternoon/evening allowing winds to diminish\nand skies to clear. This will allow temperatures to fall into \nthe upper 20s tonight, bringing the first freeze of the season \nfor most areas across northern MO. High pressure and northerly\nwinds remain present into the weekend with highs only climbing \ninto the mid-to-upper 40s on Friday and Saturday. Saturday \nmorning will be the coldest of the forecast period with lows \ndropping into the low 20s (some may even see temperatures in the\nteens), making this the first hard freeze (temperatures below \n28F) for most of the CWA.\n\nBy Sunday, upper-level ridging is expected to slide east over \nthe Central Plains, bringing height rises to the area. The \nsurface high pressure will also shift to the east and southerly \nflow and WAA will return, bringing warmer temperatures back \ninto the forecast. Sunday should see high temperatures climb \nback into the low 50s, with lows right around freezing. Each \nsubsequent day is expected to warm an additional 3-4 degF as the\nridge continues to build over the central CONUS.\n\nPrevious forecast cycles have noted a shortwave that is progged\nto move across the Central Plains on Sunday afternoon. Models \nhave continued to shift the path of the shortwave and associated\nsurface system further south. At this point, all precipitation \nfrom this system is expected to remain south of the CWA. The \nnext chance (20-30%) for precipitation comes Wednesday into \nThursday, but discrepancies between the models makes this a low \nconfidence forecast at this range.",
